course: physics
lesson: 18
title: Thunder, sirens and how fast sound travels
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sound is fast, but nowhere near as fast as light: in air it goes about one kilometre every three seconds, while light crosses the same distance almost instantly.
explain: That is why you see the flash of lightning first and hear the thunder later; every three seconds of waiting means the storm is about a kilometre away.
explain: Sound moves much faster through solid things like steel or a tight string than through air, because their tightly packed bits pass the wobble on quickly.
explain: When an ambulance races towards you its sound waves are squashed together and the siren sounds higher; as it drives away they are stretched out and it sounds lower.
explain: Loud low sounds are big pushes of air, so at a concert you can feel the drums in your chest, and very loud music for a long time can damage your ears for ever.
fact: In air sound travels about one kilometre every three seconds, while light arrives almost instantly, being about a million times faster.
fact: Counting the seconds between lightning and thunder and dividing by three gives roughly how many kilometres away the storm is.
fact: Lightning heats the air in an instant to hotter than the surface of the Sun, and the air bursts outwards with a huge sound wave, the thunder.
fact: Thunder rumbles because sound from different parts of the long lightning bolt reaches you at different times.
fact: A siren sounds higher as it comes towards you and lower as it goes away, because its waves are squashed in front and stretched out behind; astronomers use the same squashing and stretching of starlight to tell how fast a star moves towards or away from us.
fact: Sound travels many times faster in steel than in air, but railway lines are deadly, because trains come faster and more quietly than you expect, and you must never go on them.
fact: A string telephone works because a tight string carries the wobble from one cup to the other; a slack string or a finger on it stops the sound.
fact: A plane flying faster than sound piles its sound waves up into one big shock wave that is heard on the ground as a sonic boom; a cracking whip makes a tiny one.
fact: Your recorded voice sounds strange to you, though it is how others hear you, because you normally hear your voice partly through the bones of your head, which makes it sound deeper.
fact: Very loud sound for a long time damages the tiny hairs in your ears for ever, so people wear earplugs at loud concerts.
fact: Cupping your hand behind your ear gathers more sound into it, which is why foxes and rabbits have big ears that turn towards sounds.
fact: A whisper can travel round a curved wall in a whispering gallery, such as the famous one in St Paul's Cathedral in London.
exercise: Why do you see lightning before you hear the thunder? | Light is far faster than sound | Thunder happens after the lightning has ended | Your eyes work faster than your ears | Lightning is closer to you than thunder
exercise: About how far does sound travel in air every three seconds? | About one kilometre | About one metre | About one hundred kilometres | About ten kilometres
exercise: You count nine seconds between a flash and its thunder. About how far away is the storm? | About three kilometres | About nine kilometres | About one kilometre | About thirty kilometres
exercise: What makes the boom of thunder? | Lightning heats the air so fast that it bursts outwards | Clouds banging into each other | Rain hitting the ground all at once | The lightning bolt hitting the clouds
exercise: Why does thunder rumble on and on instead of being one bang? | Sound from different parts of the long bolt arrives at different times | The clouds keep bumping together | The thunder bounces off the Moon | Lightning strikes many times in one place
exercise: Why does a siren sound higher as the ambulance comes towards you? | The sound waves in front of it are squashed together | The driver turns the siren up louder as he gets close to you | Sound gets higher when it is near you | The ambulance gets louder and so higher
exercise: Why does the siren sound lower as it drives away? | Its sound waves behind it are stretched out | The siren gets tired and slows down as it goes away | The driver switches to a lower siren | Low sounds travel further than high ones
exercise: In an old cowboy film, why does a man put his ear to the rail to hear a train? | Sound travels much faster in steel than in air | Steel makes sound louder by heating it | Air carries no sound at all in the desert | The train whistle only works through rails
exercise: How does a string telephone carry your voice? | The tight string carries the wobble from cup to cup | Electricity runs along the string | Your voice travels through the air beside the string | The cups store your words and pass them on
exercise: Your friend pinches the string of a string telephone. What happens? | The sound stops | The sound gets louder | The sound gets higher | Nothing changes
exercise: What makes the loud boom when a jet breaks the sound barrier? | Its sound waves pile up into one big shock wave | The engines explode briefly | The jet hits a solid wall of air and bumps into it | The pilot fires a gun to celebrate
exercise: Why does your own voice sound strange on a recording? | You usually hear it partly through the bones of your head | Recorders always change your voice | Your voice changes as soon as you know you are being recorded | The microphone adds an accent
exercise: Why can you feel very loud music in your chest at a concert? | Loud low sounds are big pushes of air that make your body wobble | The music makes your heart beat with it | The floor shakes from people dancing | The lights make you feel the sound
exercise: What can very loud music for a long time do to your ears? | Damage their tiny hairs for ever | Make them stronger and sharper | Make them grow bigger | Nothing, ears cannot be harmed by sound
exercise: Why does cupping your hand behind your ear help you hear? | It gathers more sound into your ear | It keeps your ear warm so it works better | It blocks out all the other noise around you | It makes the sound travel a lot faster
exercise: Where is a famous whispering gallery? | St Paul's Cathedral in London | The Eiffel Tower in Paris, France | The Great Wall of China, near Beijing | Big Ben's clock face in the tower
test: no worries, thunder's like 20 seconds after the flash, storm's miles off yeah? | No, that is only about seven kilometres, still close enough to be careful | Yes, 20 seconds means hundreds of kilometres | Yes, thunder after 5 seconds is safe | No, it means the storm is right overhead
test: A cartoon shows thunder and lightning arriving together, even from far away. What is wrong? | Far away, the thunder comes later than the flash | Nothing, they always arrive together | Thunder always comes before the flash, however far away | Lightning makes no light far away
test: Why is putting your ear to a railway line a terrible idea in real life? | Trains are faster and quieter than you think, and railways are deadly | It works only in old films | The rail would freeze your ear | Steel carries no sound at all
test: A police car drives past you at speed. Which is true about its siren? | It sounds higher coming and lower going | It sounds the same the whole time | It sounds lower coming and higher going | It goes silent as it passes
test: Why do foxes and rabbits have big ears that turn? | They gather more sound from where it comes | Big ears keep them warm out in the cold snow | Big ears help them to run along much faster | Big ears are only there for showing off to others
test: ugh my voice on the video sounds well weird, is the phone broken? | No, that is how you really sound to others | Yes, phones always change voices | Yes, your voice is deeper than that | No, your voice changes when you hear it back
test: What would a stretched-out slack string do to your string telephone? | Carry the sound badly or not at all | Carry the sound twice as well as before | Make all the sounds come out much higher | Make your friend hear you a little sooner
test: A whip makes a sharp crack. What is happening at its tip? | It moves faster than sound and makes a tiny boom | The leather at the very end snaps in two with a crack | The tip of the whip hits a fly that is passing by | The handle makes the crack, and the tip makes nothing
test: The same whisper is heard on the far side of a round gallery but not across a square hall. Why? | The curved wall guides the sound round its edge | Round rooms make every sound louder by a kind of magic | Whispers travel faster in round rooms | Square halls block all whispers
test: Which is true about sound and light? | Light is about a million times faster than sound | Sound is faster than light | They travel at exactly the same speed through the air | Sound is a kind of light
test: Astronomers use the same squashed and stretched waves with light from stars. What can they learn? | How fast the stars are moving towards or away from us | How hot the stars are inside | How old the stars are | How many planets each star has
exam: Identify the principal reason for which thunder is perceived subsequent to the corresponding flash of lightning. | Light is propagated at a vastly greater speed than sound | The thunder is produced after the lightning has ceased | The ear responds more slowly than the eye | The lightning and the thunder originate in different clouds
exam: It is given that an interval of nine seconds elapses between a flash and the ensuing thunder. Ascertain the approximate distance of the discharge. | Three kilometres | Nine kilometres | One kilometre | Twenty-seven kilometres
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alteration of the pitch of a passing siren? | The pitch rises upon approach and falls upon recession | The pitch falls upon approach and rises upon recession | The pitch remains constant throughout | The pitch is determined solely by the volume
exam: State the anticipated consequence of prolonged exposure to sound of excessive intensity. | Permanent damage to the sensory hairs of the inner ear | An enhancement of the acuity of hearing | An enlargement of the external ear | No consequence of any description
